plan Persistence
title   Persistence: durable state without replaying work
status  proposed
goal
    An app can retain selected drafts and confirmed data across reloads within one app, scope and
    version. Restore never repeats a remote write, exposes partial saved state or undoes a live 
    turn.
outcomes
    O1  Configuration and storage
        runtimeSetup({ app, scope, services?, persistence?: { version } }) enables persistence.
        Without it, Flow acquires no storage and performs no restore, capture or storage I/O.
        Persistence requires the setup's scope (@plan.Runtime.O2); its absence is a type error and
        rejects untyped setups before construction, so saved data never lands in a shared partition.
        version is a required nonempty string and matches exactly. Different apps and scopes never
        read, overwrite or delete each other's data.

        Enabled persistence requires an Effect KeyValueStore service from effect/persistence;
        missing provision is a type error. Untyped missing provision fails ready with
        StorageFailure. construct() performs no I/O. Storage is available before restore; saved data
        is validated before actors start. Failed bootstrap exposes no partially restored live app
        and releases acquired resources under @plan.Runtime.O2.

        Flow supplies layerIndexedDb({ database? }) as a KeyValueStore Layer; database defaults to a
        name derived from the app id, so apps sharing an origin never collide. It preserves saved
        values, releases its connection on disposal and reports storage failures. Authors can supply
        another KeyValueStore without changing persistence selection. Public routes follow
        @plan.Package.O1; isolated Story storage follows @plan.Testing.O3.
    O2  What survives reload
        Selection defaults to false. actorRef(machine, id, { persist: true }) selects a stable
        shared actor; createActor and local React actors never persist. resource({ persist: true })
        selects confirmed resource data independently of actor selection. Transactions have no
        persist option: pending writes follow their actor. A stream's persist option retains its
        latest value only when its owning actor also persists.

        Actor state, memory, exact context bindings, issues and pending outcomes survive reload,
        together with schedule deadlines, restartOn baselines, actor revisions and, for each
        activity binding, the last value it mapped to a value outcome. After reload, value fires
        only when the confirmed value differs from that mapped value, so a fresh lookup of unchanged
        data never overwrites a restored draft. Memory uses its definition's Schema; selected
        resource and stream results require Schemas. Persisted Schemas must encode and decode
        synchronously without services; incompatible Schemas are a type error. Unsupported values
        fail encoding instead of being coerced, omitted or stringified.

        Only confirmed shared data survives; optimistic values and predictions never become a saved
        baseline. Entity projection and uncertainty repair follow @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Queued
        events (including admitted public events not yet processed), commands, executable work,
        services and stream emission buffers do not survive. Pending mapped outcomes survive for
        once-only delivery. Context selections are recomputed from the saved providers, never
        restored as independent overrides.
    O3  Complete captures and writes
        A capture contains a consistent view of selected actors and shared confirmed data: it sees
        only whole publications, never part of a turn or an actor without its store writes. It never
        starts operations, emits context events or changes live state. Explicitly disposed durable
        actors disappear from the next successful capture; a persisted actor whose retain expired
        keeps its saved record (@plan.Runtime.O3). Dormant lazy records follow
        @plan.LazyFeatures.O3.

        Every selected consumer requires its exact transitive context providers to be durable. A
        missing, local or non-durable provider rejects the whole capture, preserves the previous
        complete scope and reports NonDurableContextProvider with every failing context path and
        provider identity. Flow never substitutes a provider or silently drops the consumer.
        Incomplete context propagation delays capture and retries automatically when settled; it
        never writes a mixed or partial context view.

        A scope is saved atomically: after interruption or a torn write, reload sees one complete
        saved capture, falling back to the last good capture when necessary. Older writes never
        replace newer committed state. Ordinary writes are coalesced to the newest waiting state;
        the durability required by @O6 cannot be coalesced away. A scope admits at most 4,096
        records and 16 MiB of record data, including dormant records. Exceeding either bound rejects
        the whole capture without eviction. Shared value bounds follow @plan.Inspection.O2; encoding
        failure preserves the last good capture.

        Page hide, visibility becoming hidden and Runtime disposal request a flush. Browser exit
        flushing is best effort. Disposal waits for the last write before releasing storage and
        ownership, subject to bounded disposal in @plan.Runtime.O6. A write still pending when the
        disposal budget ends reports StorageFailure rather than hanging or claiming a completed
        flush. During Runtime disposal, a commit that settles before the final capture is saved as a
        pending mapped outcome and delivered after reload.
    O4  Restore without replay
        ready() restores a complete compatible scope before actor work starts. Restored stable
        actors are active without another ensure; ensureActor returns the restored actor and ignores
        fresh input. Their retain clock starts at ready. Ref-option conflicts follow
        @plan.Runtime.O3. Restore never runs initial again or replays old input or turns.

        Saved context bindings select the same providers and establish a silent baseline, without an
        onContext event. Saved mapped outcomes are delivered once, in saved order after readiness,
        before new external events and before continuing activities reconcile. Pending remote
        commits follow @O6; finite resource requests never resume their old execution.

        Restored resource data starts stale. A live binding can start a fresh policy-driven lookup;
        key-only entries remain passive until a live binding supplies parameters. Parameters are
        never reconstructed from a cache key. Restored unobserved entries start gcTime at ready;
        resource freshness and parameter selection follow @plan.Operations.O2. Hydration precedence
        follows @plan.ServerRendering.O5.

        Restored schedules and hidden-tab resume follow @plan.Machines.O5. Running streams retain
        their selected latest value and count, then start a fresh session after saved outcomes
        drain; no old emission replays. Terminal streams stay terminal until their activity starts
        again. Late results from old work cannot affect restored work. Lazy adoption follows
        @plan.LazyFeatures.O3; capture and restore never load lazy code.
    O5  Honest failures and forgetting
        Incompatible versions or identities and corrupt scope data report RestoreDiscarded and start
        fresh; ready succeeds. Flow never partially restores an invalid scope. Compatible shape
        changes may use Schema decode transformations; v1 has no migration API. At ready, saved
        actor records for a machine id that no eager or lazy entry declares are discarded with a
        diagnostic. A dormant record that fails to decode when its feature is adopted discards only
        that record, with a diagnostic; the feature opens fresh (@plan.LazyFeatures.O3). A storage
        read failure reports StorageFailure and disables persistence for that Runtime, so unread
        saved data is never overwritten. Missing required storage remains a setup failure.

        A failed write preserves live committed state and the last good saved capture. It never
        retroactively fails a committed actor turn. A later publication retries the newest state.
        Encoding failure never fails the turn: the turn commits, the whole write is skipped with the
        codec's code (@plan.Inspection.O2), and the last good capture stays until an encodable state
        is captured. Failures reach Runtime diagnostic observers even without mounted durable
        actors; a failing inspection sink cannot interrupt persistence. Error vocabulary and
        remediation follow @plan.Diagnostics.O2.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) waits for this scope's data to be made unrestorable and
        deleted before releasing ownership. Failed deletion reports StorageFailure and never claims
        successful forgetting. Ordinary disposal retains saved data. A Runtime running without
        persistence performs no forget I/O. Disposal failures follow @plan.Runtime.O6.
    O6  Durable attempts before remote writes
        A persisted actor's remote commit cannot invoke its adapter until storage acknowledges its
        Attempt, including id and encoded input. If acknowledgment fails, the adapter is not called;
        the occurrence settles through interrupt with StorageFailure evidence. Transient storage
        failure never silently downgrades a selected actor's commit to an undurable write.

        A crash after acknowledgment restores unknown carrying the same Attempt unless durable
        evidence proves the adapter could not have run. A saved queued label alone is insufficient:
        dispatch may have happened after that saved capture. Flow never automatically repeats a
        remote write. Already saved mapped outcomes are delivered rather than replaced by unknown.
        Explicit retry(attempt) and known-settled behavior follow @plan.Operations.O3. A later
        Runtime never reuses an Attempt identity for an unrelated write.

        Non-persisted actors, including a Runtime refused persistence under @O7, have no attempt
        durability and lose their workflow on crash. They may still execute remote commits; there is
        no cross-tab exactly-once guarantee.
    O7  A second tab runs without persistence
        At ready(), a persistence-enabled Runtime requests an immediately available Web Lock for its
        app and scope, independent of version, before accessing storage. It holds ownership through
        final flush or forgetting and cleanup; failed bootstrap releases ownership. Other apps and
        scopes remain independent.

        If a disposing Runtime in the same document owns the scope, ready waits for its release. If
        another tab or Runtime owns that scope, this Runtime runs independently without restore,
        capture, writes or deletion and reports SecondTabNoPersistence. It stays in that mode even
        after the owner closes. A new Runtime may try again. There is no tab sharing or handover.
        Where navigator.locks is absent, persistence proceeds without an ownership check. If an
        available lock request fails, report StorageFailure and run without persistence.
decisions
    D1  Persisted storage is faithful
        Saved records hold values exactly as committed, including fields and values marked
        sensitive. Storage belongs to the user's own browser profile and scope, and restore must
        reproduce drafts unchanged. Sensitivity governs only data that leaves the Runtime: exported
        evidence (@plan.Inspection.O5) and server-rendering payloads (@plan.ServerRendering.O3).
        Clearing a scope is runtime.dispose({ forget: true }).
verification
    V1  Type tests reject missing storage and invalid persisted Schemas under @O1 and @O2.
    V2  Tests prove default-off selection, inert construction and atomic bootstrap under @O1.
    V3  Tests prove complete context capture, automatic retry and disposed-draft removal under @O3.
    V4  Reload tests prove restored identity, ordered outcomes and no work replay under @O4,
        including restore-capture-restore and hydrate-persist-reload-hydrate round trips.
    V5  Crash tests prove Attempt acknowledgment before dispatch and honest unknown under @O6.
    V6  Fault tests prove atomic fallback, bounds and preservation of live state under @O3 and @O5.
    V7  Browser tests prove second-tab refusal and no-lock persistence under @O7.
    V8  Tests prove confirmed-only restore under @O2, dormant retention under @O3 and hydration
        precedence under @O4.
    V9  Browser tests prove flush, bounded disposal and honest forgetting under @O3 and @O5.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Storage key layout and identity encoding. Record chunking, capture bookkeeping and
        generation counters. Manifest headers, integrity checks and retained-generation layout.
        Write scheduling within a publishing task. IndexedDB transaction cancellation and connection
        cleanup mechanics. Diagnostic payload bounds and encoding details. Cross-runtime codec
        vectors and exact shared codec limits. Fault-injection infrastructure and browser test
        matrix.
